    'Hy4Preview(high)V1': "This online Three.js build uses full JPL secular elements and a normalized Kepler solver, with 18 moons, Halley/Encke/67P, two moving belts, and Bloom/ACES. Rings only receive the planet's shadow; atmospheres are directional rim glow. The Moon incorrectly applies its ecliptic inclination to Earth's equator; the other 17 idealized parent-relative circular orbits remain valid. Moon orbit lines additionally omit each moon's inclination, reducing path-fit credit. Density is labeled g/cm³ although calculated in kg/m³, and Venus/Uranus spin reverses twice. Halley's argument of perihelion is also treated as perihelion longitude. These independent data issues are not repeated under moon or orbit scoring. Zoom can enter bodies and reset is incomplete.",
    'Hy4Preview(high)V2': "This online Three.js build has full JPL secular elements, a robust Kepler solver, genuine Halley, moving belts, bidirectional ring shadows, and Bloom/ACES. Of 13 moons, the approximate ecliptic Moon is valid; the other 12 apply parent-equator inclinations to the ecliptic, and Triton also combines a negative period with a 156.8-degree inclination. These errors are deducted only from valid-moon evidence. Atmospheres are directional rim glow. Venus, Uranus, and Pluto combine signed spin periods with flipped axes, reducing data correctness. Radius-based safe zoom, focus, and follow work; Wide View and Now only reset camera or time, without a full reset.",
    'Hy4Preview(high)V1-TasksAssignedByOpus5': "Source and pure-function review: 2 of eight implemented moons pass the signed-normal/entity checks. Visual appearance is excluded. Both ring-shadow directions and the atmosphere integration exist; visual appearance does not reduce their score. The actual right-handed basis reverses ordinary equatorial moons, while Moon and Triton directions are correct. Satellite day values are treated as rotation hours.",
