    'GPT6Astra(Light)V1-TasksAssignedByOpus5': { title: 'Interstellar · Real-Time Solar Observatory' , note: "Source and pure-function review: 0 of eight implemented moons pass the signed-normal/entity checks. Visual appearance is excluded. The actual right-handed basis and positive local Z reverse the ordinary moons; the Triton supplementary-angle workaround also gives the wrong direction, and the Moon is retrograde. All eight fail the signed direction requirement."},
    'Opus5(Low)V1-TasksAssignedByOpus5': {"title":"Real-Time Solar System Model","note":"Native WebGL2 implements real JPL secular elements, a J2000 epoch, robust Kepler solving and proper planet handedness. Its220-sample uniform-M Halley path deviates by up to24.088988 display units from the compressed physical curve at the default date, versus a0.9-unit comet radius; the perihelion gap gives partial path fit without a fatal classification. Only Triton has a valid156.9-degree directed orbit among eight moons. The Moon is174.855 degrees and six positive-period regular moons run retrograde in their proper frames, receiving zero quality without repeated data deductions. Two-way analytic ring shadows, moving belts, Bloom/ACES and impact-parameter density/forward-scattering atmospheres work. Venus/Uranus/Pluto double-flip negative spin and inverted axes, and Halley’s period predicts2061-06-08 while public text says July. Drag suppresses accidental picking; focus and spherical follow work. Minimum zoom0.35 can enter bodies; pause stops physics but there is no complete reset. HDR has anRGBA8 fallback and up-to4x MSAA. Unified runtime checks report no errors.","issue":""},
    'GPT5.6Sol(high)V1': {"title":"HELIOS · Dynamic Solar-System Observatory","note":"Offline WebGL2 implements Kepler motion, Bloom, ACES, an asteroid belt and focus tracking. Planetary phases start at perihelion rather than a real epoch. Only the Moon among eight drawn satellites has a valid reference plane. Rings lack mutual shadows and the atmosphere is rim lighting. Surface spin conflicts with orbital direction; reset affects the camera only.","issue":""},
    'DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731': {"title":"Solar System Simulation","note":"Nine bodies use focused ellipses, Kepler solving and oriented orbital elements. Several J2000 M0 values are mean longitudes and the epoch starts half a day early, so epoch credit is partial. The independent 5.145-degree, 27.322-day Moon is valid. Rings lack physical shadows. Belt CPU arrays are changed without marking GPU upload, leaving the displayed particles static. Bloom and OutputPass ACES work; atmospheres are sprite glows. Three independent comet trails exist but their head sprites never receive their orbital positions, reducing integrity. Retrograde axes and negative spin rates double-flip. Planet bodies expose sphere while picking reads mesh, causing hover/click errors; sidebar target tracking still works. Pause works, reset only restores the camera, and float postprocessing has no fallback or scene MSAA. The seven-unit Sun completely encloses Mercury, Venus and Earth: their maximum solar distance plus body radius is2.367/3.863/5.067. This reduces integrity without a fatal classification.","issue":""},
    'DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-TasksAssignedByOpus5': {"title":"Solar System · Real-Time Orbital Model","note":"Planetary JPL/Kepler motion, the actual Halley orbit, bidirectional ring shadows, moving belts and postprocessing work in this offline native WebGL2 sample. The Moon incorrectly uses Earth’s equatorial frame; Triton’s signed period in the actual left-handed frame produces 23.1°. The other six moons are valid, including Charon. Uniform mean-anomaly sampling with 384 points misses the high-eccentricity Halley curve near perihelion. Lunar heliocentric/Earth-distance readouts do not use actual lunar positions, and spin pole/sign conventions are inconsistent. Desktop drag release can trigger the separate click picker, which also omits the FOV projection scale. Zoom can enter large bodies. List focus and continuous follow work; Now resets only the date.","issue":""},
    'DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-V2-TasksAssignedByOpus5': {"title":"Solar System · Real-Time Motion Model","note":"Local WebGL2 has an HDR fallback, bloom, ACES, atmosphere and moving belts; rings have only one shadow direction. JPL/Kepler propagation and Halley work. A 240-point true-anomaly path omits its closing segment, so path agreement is partial. Only Triton has a valid directed moon orbit. Both frame and drive schedule themselves, while each drive call invokes frame and adds another permanent chain. Runtime pending RAF callbacks grew from 38 to 71. This is an L2 long-run core failure, assessed as unfinished T4 without the former numerical cap. Spin orientation and mass formatting have independent data errors. Focus holds an array replaced on the next frame, zoom can enter a body, and Now does not reset the full state.","issue":"L2: two self-scheduling RAF functions continually add permanent render chains, producing unbounded long-run rendering work; focus stores a stale position."},
    'DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-V3-TasksAssignedByOpus5': {"title":"Real-Time Solar System Model","note":"JPL/Keplerian planetary motion, the actual Halley, bidirectional ring shadows, moving belts, HDR/Bloom/ACES and atmospheres work; the old overexposed appearance causes no deduction. Directed normals validate only Triton; the Moon and six other moons have reversed motion. Body and ribbon paths do not fully coincide, so path fit is partial. Spins continue while paused, and satellite display-space coordinates are reported as AU and Earth distances; these data errors are deducted separately from orbital direction. Drag suppression works, but picking mixes device and CSS pixels. Focus continuously pulls zoom back and the minimum allows entry into bodies. Follow works and Now changes only the date.","issue":""},
    'DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813-V1': { title: 'Solar System · Real-Time Motion Model' , note: "This online Three.js build has measured eccentricities, J2000 phases, Kepler motion, two moving belts, and Bloom/ACES. Its node/inclination Euler rotations use the wrong order, while orbit lines apply a perihelion argument that bodies omit. Mercury and Mars are about 6."},
    'DeepSeekV4Pro0813(Max)V1-TasksAssignedByOpus5': { title: 'Real-Time Solar System Model' , note: "Source and pure-function review: 2 of eight implemented moons pass the signed-normal/entity checks. Visual appearance is excluded. Analytical planetary paths agree. Ring shadow rays lack a forward-intersection check. Moon and Triton are valid, but ordinary equatorial moon directions are reversed. The clock still follows wall time while paused."},
