    'DeepSeekV4Pro0813(Max)V2-TasksAssignedByOpus5': { title: 'Real-Time Solar System Model' , note: "Source and pure-function review: 2 of eight implemented moons pass the signed-normal/entity checks. Visual appearance is excluded. The planet-shadow attenuation on rings is inverted. Moon and Triton are valid; ordinary equatorial moon directions are reversed. Purely visual integrity deductions are removed."},
    'DeepSeekV4.1Flash0910(Max)V1': { title: 'Solar System · Real-Time WebGL2 Simulation' , note: "Self-contained native WebGL2 implements JPL secular/Kepler planet orbits, 15 rendered moons, bidirectional ring shadows, moving main/Kuiper belts, Bloom/ACES and camera tracking. Atmosphere combines Fresnel rim lighting with an approximate forward term, without optical-path integration, and receives partial credit. Triton is double-reversed by a negative period plus 156."},
    'DeepSeekV4.1Flash0910(Max)V1-TasksAssignedByOpus5': { title: 'Real-Time Solar System Model' , note: "Source and pure-function review: 1 of eight implemented moons pass the signed-normal/entity checks. Visual appearance is excluded. Planetary paths and rendering work. The Moon reference mapping is wrong, while only Triton has a valid signed direction. Display units leak into AU distance fields, with an inconsistent Halley date."},
    'DeepSeekProMax-TasksAssignedByOpus5': {"title":"Real-Time Solar System Model — DeepSeek V4 Pro (Max)","note":"This offline native WebGL2 sample uses real JPL/Keplerian planetary and Halley motion, moving belts, Bloom/ACES and atmospheric shells. Rings have only partial shadowing. All eight moon implementations fail reference/direction checks: the Moon’s positive world-Z phase is retrograde, while the other moons treat the raw ecliptic pole as a world-space pole without the required mapping. Cached static orbit elements and uniform mean-anomaly samples prevent full path fit. Planetary spin axes also mix these frames, and Halley’s stated return date differs from its model; these receive a modest data deduction. Drag release can mispick, zoom can enter bodies, focus retains a stale position snapshot instead of following, and Now resets only the date.","issue":""},
    'DeepSeek_V4_Pro_high-1': {"title":"Solar System","note":"Online Three.js implements eight illustrative circular planet paths, a moving asteroid belt and Bloom. Saturn has transparent lit geometry without physical ring shadows. There are no real orbital elements, Keplerian timing or epoch. The Moon inherits a randomly tilted, spinning Earth mesh and is invalid; this is not deducted again under data. Planet spins and tilts are arbitrary, including incorrect Venus rotation. Only drag and zoom are available, with a safe five-unit minimum; there is no focus, follow, pause or reset. Half-float postprocessing has no fallback or scene MSAA. Actual UnrealBloomPass output tone-maps the base image through MeshBasicMaterial, then adds Bloom without mapping the combined result; ACES receives0.4 partial credit.","issue":""},
    'DeepSeek_V4_Pro_high-2': {"title":"Solar System Motion Model","note":"Eight planets use approximate periods on circular paths with inclinations and random phase, without Keplerian timing or epoch. Static path lines omit the complete phase-dependent Euler transform. A three-coordinate call to setXY discards its third argument and collapses Saturn’s ring to a line. The moving belt and Bloom work; the composer has no OutputPass and applies ACES only partially. Atmospheres are rim glows. There are no moons or comets. Spins are random and ignore the simulation multiplier. There is no pause control; R only restores the camera. Focus and follow only steer the look-at target. Half-float postprocessing has no fallback or scene MSAA. Actual UnrealBloomPass output tone-maps the base image through MeshBasicMaterial, then adds Bloom without mapping the combined result; ACES receives0.4 partial credit.","issue":""},
    'DeepSeek_V4_Pro_high-3': {"title":"Solar System","note":"Eight planets use illustrative circles and approximate periods, with matching path lines but no eccentric elements, Keplerian timing, orbital nodes or epoch. Rings and the static belt are partial; direct ACES works without Bloom, and the atmosphere is a transparent shell. The Moon remains in a zero-inclination ecliptic plane without a node and is invalid. Planet spins use arbitrary shared rates. Click focus flies to a fixed snapshot without continuous follow. Zero speed leaves spin running and reset only restores the camera. Direct rendering needs no float color attachment and enables canvas MSAA.","issue":""},
    'GLM5.2Max-TasksAssignedByOpus5': {"title":"Real-Time Solar System Model","note":"Local WebGL2 includes an HDR fallback, multilevel bloom, ACES and functional atmospheric density. The only ring-shadow ray test reverses attenuation, and no ring shadow is cast onto the planet. JPL elements and Kepler propagation work, while fixed J2000 orbit buffers provide partial path agreement. Six of eight moons are valid: the Moon has a directed inclination of 95.145°, and Triton is flipped to 23.1°. Some retrograde spin axes and signed spin periods conflict. Dragging can also trigger picking, zoom can enter a body, focusing stores a position without following, and Now only resets the date. No fatal failure is established.","issue":""},
    'GLM5.2Max': {"title":"Solar System","note":"Eight planets follow illustrative circles with approximate orbital periods and matching paths, but no Kepler timing, orbital orientation or epoch. The moving belt, Bloom and OutputPass ACES work; rings lack physical shadows and atmospheres are Fresnel rims. The Moon has no inclination/node and an arbitrary period, so it is invalid. Spin periods are inverted twice, making Mercury rotate 58.6 times faster than Earth; Venus and Uranus also double-flip axis and signed spin. Minimum distance eight is inside the Sun’s radius twelve. Focus flights work, subsequent follow only steers the target. Zero speed stops planets, while reset only restores the camera. Float postprocessing has no fallback or scene MSAA.","issue":""},
